From cf4dd44c994c2d4d5d3c9df1409ce6d9578746f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Your Name <gamefroggy16@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 21:40:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] sd-boot: accept input from XInput gamepads

The boot menu can only be driven from EFI_SIMPLE_TEXT_INPUT_EX_PROTOCOL, and an
XInput pad is not reachable that way: it presents a vendor-specific interface
(class 0xff, subclass 0x5d, protocol 0x01) rather than USB HID, so no firmware
publishes a text-input protocol for it.

UsbBusDxe does install EFI_USB_IO_PROTOCOL on every interface it enumerates
whatever the class, so claim the interface and drive its interrupt IN endpoint
ourselves. Reports are decoded into sd-boot key codes and pushed onto a small
queue; the callback signals an event that console_key_read() adds to the set it
already waits on, so no polling loop is needed.

Matching on the interface triple rather than a VID/PID list means any XInput pad
works, 8BitDo 2.4GHz dongles included, and it keeps us off interfaces the
firmware is itself driving -- relaxing the filter to HID was enough to claim the
firmware USB keyboard endpoint during testing.

Presses are edge triggered, since a held direction would otherwise run the
selection away. Transfers are cancelled before chainloading: the firmware would
keep calling a callback that points into sd-boot memory. Xbox One style GIP pads
are detected and named in the status screen but not driven; they need a power-on
packet and a different report layout.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
